LinkedIn  is the world's largest professional networking platform, with over 1 billion members, designed to connect professionals to make them more productive and successful. It acts as a digital resume, a networking hub, a job search engine, and a platform for building a personal brand.
LinkedIn is designed to help users build their professional identity and engage with their network. How I plan to use linkedin over the next four years:
·	Networking: Connecting with colleagues, industry peers, mentors, and potential employers to build meaningful relationships.
·	Personal Branding: Showcasing skills, achievements, experience, and endorsements to stand out in a competitive job market.
·	Job Hunting: Accessing a vast repository of job listings, researching companies, and using the "Open to Work" feature to signal availability to recruiters.
·	Content Sharing & Thought Leadership: Publishing articles, posts, and videos to share insights, industry trends, and perspectives to establish authority.
·	Skill Development: Using LinkedIn Learning (formerly Lynda.com) to take courses and acquire new skills.

GitHub is a cloud-based platform that acts as a hosting service for Git repositories, enabling developers to store, manage, track changes to code, and collaborate on software development projects. As of 2025, it is the world's largest source code host, serving as an industry-standard tool for software development, version control, and project management. 
How I plan to use Github over the next four years:
·	Version Control: Uses Git to track changes over time, allowing developers to revert to previous versions of code if mistakes are made.
·	Collaboration: Facilitates team collaboration, allowing multiple developers to work on the same codebase simultaneously without overwriting each other's work, using branches and pull requests.
·	Code Hosting: Serves as a centralized repository (cloud storage) for code, ensuring backup and accessibility from anywhere.
·	Open Source & Networking: Hosts millions of open-source projects and functions as a social network for developers to showcase work, follow others, and contribute to projects.
·	Automation (CI/CD): Features like GitHub Actions enable automated testing, building, and deploying of code.
·	Project Management: Includes tools for tracking bugs, tasks, and features through Issues, Projects, and Wikis.
·	Portfolio Building: Allows developers to showcase their work to potential employers.

Kaggle is a prominent, Google-owned online community and platform for data science and machine learning (ML) practitioners. It acts as a "proving ground" for AI talent, hosting over 15 million users and providing tools to explore, analyze, and build models on real-world datasets. 
How I plan to use Kaggle over the next four years:
·	Competitions: Organizations sponsor challenges ranging from predicting medical outcomes to advanced image classification. Participants build models, check leaderboards, and win prizes or recognition.
·	Public Datasets & Notebooks: It hosts a massive repository of datasets and Kaggle Notebooks (formerly Kernels), a browser-based integrated development environment (IDE) that provides free access to CPUs, GPUs, and TPUs for coding in Python/R.
·	Learning & Skill Development: Kaggle offers hands-on courses, tutorials, and community-shared code to practice data cleaning, visualization, and algorithm development.
·	Collaboration & Networking: The community forum allows users to discuss techniques, share code, form teams for competitions, and learn from other's submissions.
·	Career Advancement: Winning or placing high in competitions and building a high-level profile (Expert/Master/Grandmaster) can showcase skills to potential employers.
